atmospheric-pressure-answer-why = While the nozzle is open, air comes into the syringe after the plunger and presses on it from inside as much as from outside — it pulls easily. With the nozzle shut, there is almost nothing inside to press, and the air outside pushes on the plunger with F = p·S. But a little air always stays in the nozzle. As the plunger goes up, that air spreads over the whole syringe and presses from inside the weaker the more room it has. So the force grows the farther the plunger is pulled and comes near p·S, but does not reach it: the syringe’s pressure comes out a little less than the barometer shows.
